  "synonym_discrimination": {
    "words": [
      "head-n",
      "boss-n",
      "leader-n",
      "manager-n",
      "chief-n"
    ],
    "note": "**Head** is the person in charge of a school, a department, or a company, and is often part of a title: *head of sales*. Your **boss** is the person you work for; the word is informal. A **leader** is the person that a group, a party, or a country follows, whether chosen, appointed, or simply obeyed; the word is about influence more than about a job title. A **manager** runs a team, a shop, or a project. **Chief** appears mostly in fixed titles: *chief of police*, *chief executive*."
  },
  "usage_note": "As a title, **head** comes before the noun without an article: *head coach*; *She is head of the department.* **Head teacher** and **headmaster** are British; American schools have a principal. **Heads**, the side of a coin, takes a singular verb: *Heads is my choice.*",
  "learner_errors": [],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [
    {
      "slug": "neck-n",
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "slug": "brain-n",
      "note": null
    }
  ],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Other languages may use the body word for some of these senses, the mind, the leader, or the top, but rarely for all: the head of a company may be a director, and the head of a table or a page may need the word for end or top. **Heads** (coin) and **a head** (per person) usually need separate words.",
    "grammar": "In counting uses **head** has no plural: *a head taller*, *fifty head of cattle*, *ten dollars a head*. Before a noun it works like an adjective and takes no article: *head coach*, *head office*.",
    "culture": "Nodding the head means yes and shaking it from side to side means no in English-speaking countries; in some other cultures these movements have different meanings, so the collocations **nod** and **shake** need care.",
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The vowel is the short **e** of *bed*, although the spelling **ea** usually stands for the long vowel of *eat*; *bread*, *dead*, and *ready* follow the same pattern."
  },
